#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221196 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff3452-19b6-566a-8b39-ad880dee8858" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of the Horizontal Scaling on Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "b8658f47-7f42-530a-a62e-1cc3144059aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21835" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb R.$ \nGiven that $L=2$ and $f(x) = \\cosh{(x+1)}$, find the $4$th cosine and the $4$th sine coefficients." :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k \\neq 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi.$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\na_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+\nb_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and $f(x)=\\cosh(x+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x, \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221197 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "ba1cc59e-66ed-58fc-a494-b949c7ed0a88" :question/math-academy-id "e-21836" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_0.$" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({2}^{3}-2\\cdot {2}^{2}+2)-((-2)^{3}-2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-(-18)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "823fc00b-48af-5e79-9dcc-5e519e81cd4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21837" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+4$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_3.$" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-5x+4$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\cdot (-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }[(-5(2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-(-5(-2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi )]+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{20}{9{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e95e33-347b-57a5-99b1-b6350c6a1b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e95e33-347b-57a5-99b1-b6350c6a1b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311966} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b992e7c-efa0-59cc-a0fb-844e1ecd2b0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ed7ffbf-06f9-50a7-bc59-05cb4e317a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-340873" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2.$ Also, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,2)$ and $f(x)=-1$ for $x\\in (-2,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264561c5-9799-51c9-a009-d1202a71c2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264561c5-9799-51c9-a009-d1202a71c2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315334} :answer-field/id #uuid "79eafde2-60a3-502c-bcb3-a8d0d2d51c6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e12f9894-53cb-5435-b393-ea7e08594626" :question/math-academy-id "q-341789" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ The Fourier series of the $2$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2+\\frac{5}{2}+1)-((-2)+\\frac{5}{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c493bcc-7040-54a8-b5d3-429d4cf6c165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c493bcc-7040-54a8-b5d3-429d4cf6c165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372796} :answer-field/id #uuid "66bca651-fc67-586c-a6ac-fc1254c6e1f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2e5c8de-82af-59f7-9831-0d0c36257889" :question/math-academy-id "q-341792" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{6}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{6}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2{x}^{2}+7x+5$ for $x\\in [-6,6).$ The Fourier series of the $12$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=6$ and $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+7x+5$ for $x\\in [-6,6).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}(2{x}^{2}+7x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+5x]}_{-6}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}((\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}+5\\cdot 6)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot (-6)^{2}+5\\cdot (-6))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(300-(-48)) \\\\  & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
